WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.970
They say history repeats itself -- I'm a big
believer in this theory, which is why I try

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:13.340
to spend more time on this show talking about
big ideas and political philosophies rather

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.090
than day to day political rumblings.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:21.360
Most of our political debates and media coverage
has devolved into catchy sound bites and zingers,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:27.050
usually staged around some sort of publicity
stunt instead of true substantive debate.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.760
Even protesting these days seems to be more
about screaming and hashtagging than making

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.440
a coherent argument.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.190
Just think back to the election itself -- did
the candidates and media spend more time on

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.410
the issues, or on the circus?

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.610
I think the answer is pretty obvious.

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:46.100
Understanding political philosophies helps
to guide us through the endless noise that

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.269
comes out of our political machine.

00:00:48.269 --> 00:00:52.059
This is why I've tried to talk about issues
like gay marriage in a context which shows

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:56.420
you can be liberal and for gay marriage because
you want gay people to be treated equally

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.870
under the law, or you can be libertarian and
for gay marriage because you don't want the

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.960
government involved in a contract between
two consenting adults.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:10.630
If you understand the philosophic underpinnings
of liberalism and libertarianism in this case,

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.490
it can bring you to the same conclusion.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:17.940
Without understanding political philosophy,
your thoughts on any day to day issue will

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.570
be guided more by your feelings than by facts.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.720
We see plenty of facts over feelings in our
public discourse these days, and it's partly

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.200
why we are so divided as a nation.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.780
By the way, that isn't to say feelings don't
matter at all, because we're humans and they

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:38.420
do, but the way we are governed has to be
based on what is empirically true, not just

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.510
what makes us feel good.

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:44.840
Beyond just knowing political philosophies,
knowing history is an incredibly important

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:48.560
part of having a well rounded understanding
of how the world works.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:52.619
Think of how many people you talk to who claim
to know everything about what tax system is

00:01:52.619 --> 00:01:55.689
right or what foreign policy is best.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:02:00.210
Next time ask them something specific, like
"tell me the reasons a flat tax is wrong"

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.869
or "remind me the history of the middle east
during the ottoman empire."

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:09.780
Everyone on television seems to have the need
to pretend they know everything about everything.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.570
These are the people you have to watch out
for the most.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:18.280
If history does repeat itself, I'm not exactly
sure what it's repeating right now, and no,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:21.770
I don't think we're repeating Nazi Germany
even thought it's a very trendy phrase to

00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:23.280
say that these days.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.370
We truly are at a unique crossroads in American
politics, which I can't directly compare to

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.900
any other time.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:36.200
It's not just America having two diametrically
opposed political parties between the Left

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.680
and the Right.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.710
With Donald Trump in the White House, we now
have a presidential wild card who has turned

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.830
political alignments on their head.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:50.200
For example, Democrats are now suddenly arguing
for states rights because they don't like

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.870
all the federal power Trump has with his series
of executive orders.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:59.290
This political chaos is also pushing massive
amounts of people sick of the extremes on

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.010
both sides into some sort of amorphous middle.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.980
There doesn't seem to be a political leader
for this New Center, nor anyone mainstream

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.230
talking about it, but mark my words it's here.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:15.280
The key to this New Center's success will
be who sees it first and turns it into a political

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.280
movement.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.180
Whether that means a new party, or a reforming
of one of the two major parties, or something

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.070
else altogether, that remains to be seen.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:28.870
On top of this new political alignment, we
also have access to information like never

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:29.880
before.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:34.881
As I often talk about, we walk around with
the world in our pockets --- right now, you

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:40.140
can connect with someone across the globe
just as easily as with someone across the

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.140
street.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:45.690
The genius and power of the internet's ability
to change our physical world can't be over

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:46.690
stated.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.830
This connectivity has started revolutions
in the middle east and shined light on the

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:54.020
inner workings of our government via Wikileaks.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:04:00.260
But at the same time, so much of the information
is not just noise alone, but noise intended

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:05.440
to deflect, confuse, and upset you, made by
people who want to profit off you or outright

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.270
control you.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:11.761
Of course if our mainstream media had done
their job for the past 8 years, which is to

00:04:11.761 --> 00:04:16.410
be the guardian of truth in our society, then
maybe the importance of the internet wouldn't

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.340
be so vital to our functioning democracy in
2017.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:25.130
Unfortunately cable and network news dropped
the ball at almost every corner and here we

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.130
are.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:30.350
They cozied to power instead of keeping it
in check, they colluded with the people they

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.980
were supposed to keep watch over, and they
partied with the very politicians they were

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.970
supposed to be reporting on.

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:42.200
Now, suddenly the mainstream media is trying
to get its mojo back by being hard on Trump,

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:45.270
but I would argue it's basically too late
to do that.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:49.290
Actually it's kinda sad watching them flail
around knowing that less and less people are

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:50.940
paying attention to them.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:54.640
Even right now there's talk they they are
going to cancel the White House correspondents

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.820
dinner because so many in the media dislike
trump.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:02.420
Think how insane that is... when they like
the President they can party and drink with

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:03.420
him...

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:05.900
now they don't, so the jig is up.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.460
Maybe we needed them to guard the guy they
liked as much as the guy they don't like.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:15.960
At the same time we more and more cater the
News to ourselves, we don't listen to pundits

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.880
we like, we impugn the motives of anyone we
disagree with and we block friends and family

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.720
who dare have a different opinion.

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:29.210
All of this is exactly why we need to know
history and philosophy more now than ever.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:33.090
We need to understand where we came from so
we can know where we're going.

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:37.460
We need to know why the founding fathers wrote
the constitution the way they did.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:42.580
We need to understand why they yearned for
freedom and liberty in a way that seems archaic

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:48.180
to us now, but is possibly the most important
lesson we could learn in 2017.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.070
With all this in mind, I can't think of a
better guest for this week as we continue

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.690
our partnership with the fine folks over at
Learn Liberty.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:06:01.000
Dan Carlin is a radio and TV personality who
hosts two podcasts, 'Common Sense' and 'Hardcore

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.000
History.'

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.790
As a self described political martian, Dan
uses thought experiments to try to make some

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:08.710
sense of the modern world.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:13.700
On his podcast Hardcore History he hosts long,
well thought out presentations of historical

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:19.250
events set to the backdrop of how we might
view these events thought a current day lens.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:25.030
While he isn't technically a historian, he's
proven himself to be incredibly adept at storytelling

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:30.110
history in a way that offers some modern day
answers to historical events.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:34.900
If history repeats itself then lets make the
next repeat a little more enlightened... and

00:06:34.900 --> 00:06:37.800
a little less painful... than the last go
around.

